<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#hindu-arabic-numeral-system">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hindu–Arabic numeral system</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Hindu-Arabic numerals</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a>–Arabic numeral system is the decimal place-value system of writing numbers with ten digits, including zero, used throughout the world today. It developed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> by the 6th or 7th century, was taken up by mathematicians of the Islamic world such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-khwarizmi">al-Khwarizmi</a>, and reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fibonacci">Fibonacci</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liber-abaci">Liber Abaci</a> of 1202 did much to spread it among European merchants.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hindu-arabic-numeral-system.jpg" alt="Hindu–Arabic numeral system" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Cmglee · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:EgyptphoneKeypad.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
